//! The MML language as plain text, one source for HELP's topic "A SONG IN
//! MML" (which puts the IDE's buttons before it) and for `voxcade mcp
//! --music`'s `music_words syntax`: a macro, so `concat!` can join it to
//! HELP's own sentences at compile time.

/// The language: settings, instruments, channels, notes, voices, layers,
/// samples.
macro_rules! mml_language {
    () => {
        "Lines starting with # are settings (#tempo is beats a minute, 1 to 999, #meter the beats in a bar, #loop 33 80 plays the song from its start once and then bars 33 to 80 round and round, counted from 1, what comes after them never reached, #loop 33 goes round from bar 33 to the end and #loop no plays it once; a note held over the loop's end is let go there, its release ringing on as the loop begins again), lines starting with @ make an instrument (a wave, square, saw, triangle, sine or noise, or sample, below, then options like attack, decay, sustain and release, written name=number; a number out of its range is a mistake saying the range, a word one slip off says which it meant), and a line like A: is a channel of notes, one voice each (@lead in it picks the instrument; without one it plays a plain square): a note takes the voice over from the one before, which fades out under it in 5 ms if it still sounds, and a rest lets it ring out. Notes are c d e f g a b (or C D E F G A B), + or - for sharp and flat (++ or -- twice), then a length (4 a quarter, 8 an eighth, a dot makes it longer), & holds a note on by another length (c4&8, or c4&c8: the same pitch), r a rest, o4 the octave (0 to 9), < and > one octave down and up, l8 the default length, v0 to v15 the volume, [ ... ]3 repeats three times, $name = ... a short piece to use again, | a bar line (ignored). A channel may go on over several lines; a mistake says the line it is on. Every channel should be as long as the others, or the short ones fall silent before the song starts over. Name the file in c.audio.music and play it by its file's name, without the folder and .mml: music:play(\"drive\"). decay in an instrument is the fall from the peak to the sustain level, not the fade to silence a sound file's decay is. An instrument can sing: singer or voice in place of a wave (@vox singer ...). singer is the clear one, made for words to be understood; voice the rougher one of before, good for short shouts. Then a text in quotes before a note is the syllable that note sings, its phonemes split by spaces, in small letters: \"l a\" c4, \"s ae ng\" e4 (consonants, one vowel, consonants). The vowel starts on the note's beat, the consonants before it sound just before the beat, the ones after it end where the note is let go. A voice note without a text holds the vowel before it (a melisma). The phonemes: vowels a ae e eh i ih o u ax er oe ue, diphthongs aw ow ai oi ei, consonants m n ng l r w j f v s z sh ch x h rr t k p d g b (ae as in cat, eh bed, ih bit, ax the a in sofa, er an unstressed German -er as in Schwester, oe and ue the German ö and ü, ng sing, j yes, sh she, ch the German ich, x the German ach, rr a rolled r as sung; singer sings r as the German r, voice as the English one). A voice also takes consonants: its consonants' hiss, bursts and breath against its vowels, in decibels (-24 to 12, 0 as they are). Every instrument, a voice too, takes drive (decibels, 0 to 36: pushed into a soft clipper, 12 rough, 20 a shout through a megaphone with filter=bandpass cutoff=1150; drive_end makes it slide on every note, evenly in decibels over drive_time seconds, attack and decay unless written: drive=18 drive_end=3 a kick that strikes hard and dies away clean, the clipper then working the whole note, 0 a gentle rounding). A square, saw, triangle or sine takes unison: so many oscillators at once (1 to 7) spread evenly from detune semitones below the note to as far above (0 to 1, 0.1 unless written), as loud together as one: @pad saw unison=5 detune=0.2 a thick pad, the envelope, the filter and drive still one for them all. Every line, a + or = line too, has an LFO, a slow wave that moves things: lfo_cutoff the filter's cutoff that many semitones up and down (0 to 60; the line needs a filter), lfo_volume the volume down at its lowest, never up (0 to 1, 1 to silence: a tremolo), lfo_speed how often it turns in a second from each note's start (5 unless written) or lfo_beats one turn in so many of the song's beats instead, on the song's beat and with its tempo (1/16 to 64: lfo_beats=0.5 a wobble each eighth), and lfo_wave its wave: sine, triangle, square, saw (falling, a pump) or noise (a new random value each turn). @wob saw filter=lowpass cutoff=400 resonance=0.5 lfo_beats=0.5 lfo_cutoff=24 is a wobble bass and a bit crusher, crush_rate (the sound held at that many Hz, 6000 the speech of old games) and crush_bits (1 to 16 bits). A drum is often several sounds at once: lines starting with + right under an instrument are more layers of it (four with its own line at most), each a wave with words of its own, every note sounding them all, each fading by its own envelope; one line starting with = after them is what they go through together: drive, volume, filter, cutoff, resonance, crush_rate and crush_bits (music:mix turns drive, cutoff and resonance there where the = line has them, the other words on the first layer). pitch_start begins every note at a pitch in Hz that falls fast to the note's own (pitch_decay the seconds in which it goes about two thirds of the way; not with sweep), and curve=exp lets decay and release die away as a drum does, their time then the fall to -60 dB, a thousandth. An instrument can play a recording, a sample: a WAV file in the folder samples (PCM, 16 or 24 bit, mono or stereo, 22050 to 48000 Hz, 10 seconds at most, all of a game's 64 MB at most), written @break sample name=amen root=c4 start=0 end=1.92 loop=yes: name is its file's name without .wav, root the note at which it plays at its own speed (c4 unless written; a higher note plays it faster and shorter, a lower one slower and longer), start and end the seconds of it that play (all of it unless written), loop=yes that stretch round and round while the note is held, reverse=yes backwards. The envelope, the filters, drive and the crusher work on it as on any wave (crush_rate=12000 crush_bits=12 sounds like an early sampler), and a sample can be a layer: + sample name=thump. music:mix turns its root (\"g3\"), start and [\"end\"] from the channel's next note: a break cut up as the game plays. Also: t90 sets the tempo from there on in a channel, q1 to q8 how much of each note sounds before its release (8 all of it), p-100 to p100 pans a channel from left to right, #title names the song, #layer D C 0.35 lets channels D and C sound only while a game's music.intensity is at least 0.35, and ; begins a comment that runs to the line's end."
    };
}

pub(crate) use mml_language;

/// The language as text.
pub const LANGUAGE: &str = mml_language!();
